<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vakataka dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 250–500</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> · Also known as Vākāṭaka</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Vakataka dynasty was an ancient Indian dynasty that arose in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> in the mid-3rd century CE and became the most important successor of the Satavahanas there, contemporary with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Guptas</a> in the north. Little is known of its founder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vindhyashakti">Vindhyashakti</a> (r. 250–270); expansion began under his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pravarasena-i">Pravarasena I</a>, after whom the family is thought to have divided into branches, two of them known. The Gupta emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-ii">Chandragupta II</a> married his daughter into the dynasty. The Vakatakas were patrons of art and literature: the Buddhist caves of Ajanta were built under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harishena-vakataka">Harishena</a> (c. 475–500). The end of the dynasty is unknown, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a> later filled the vacuum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vakataka-dynasty.jpg" alt="Vakataka dynasty" width="330" height="499" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ajanta_Padmapani.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Vakatakas are believed to have ruled a state that stretched from the southern edges of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> in the north to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tungabhadra-river">Tungabhadra River</a> in the south, and from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a> in the west to the edges of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhattisgarh">Chhattisgarh</a> in the east. Scholars disagree about where the family began. Ramaprasad Chanda pointed to an inscription at Amaravati, dated to around the middle of the third century CE, that mentions a householder named Vakataka, and V. V. Mirashi developed this into a theory of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> origin.</p><p>Ajay Mitra Shastri instead placed the homeland in the Vindhya region, on the evidence of Puranic traditions. The epigraphist K. V. Ramesh argues that the name is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskritisation">Sanskritisation</a> of a local place-name meaning forest, and that the family was of local, non-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> origin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Almost nothing is known of the founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vindhyashakti">Vindhyashakti</a>. The Ajanta inscription of Cave XVI calls him the banner of the Vakataka family and says he added to his power by fighting great battles and had a large cavalry. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pravarasena-i">Pravarasena I</a> was the first Vakataka ruler to call himself Samrat, or universal ruler, and he fought wars with the Naga kings. He performed several Vedic sacrifices, including four horse sacrifices.</p><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a> he had four sons, and it is generally believed that the family then split into four branches, of which only two are known: the Pravarapura-Nandivardhana branch, which kept marriage ties with the Imperial Guptas, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatsagulma">Vatsagulma</a> branch, founded by his son Sarvasena, with its capital at Vatsagulma, the present Washim.</p><p>The Gupta emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-ii">Chandragupta II</a> married his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prabhavatigupta">Prabhavatigupta</a> into the Nandivardhana branch, and after the early death of her husband <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudrasena-ii">Rudrasena II</a> she acted as regent for her two sons. Her son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pravarasena-ii">Pravarasena II</a> composed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/setubandha">Setubandha</a> in Maharashtri Prakrit, founded a new capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pravarapura">Pravarapura</a> and left the largest number of copperplate grants of any Vakataka ruler, seventeen in all. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatsagulma">Vatsagulma</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harishena-vakataka">Harishena</a>, who reigned c. 475–500, is said by the Ajanta inscription to have conquered Avanti, Kosala, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a>, Andhra, Lata, Trikuta and Kuntala.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harishena">Harishena</a>, caves XVI, XVII and XIX at Ajanta, two viharas and a chaitya, were excavated and decorated with painting and sculpture, and the art historian Walter M. Spink attributes most of the rock-cut monuments of Ajanta to his reign.</p><p>The end of the dynasty is unknown. Two further rulers followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harishena">Harishena</a>, and the Kalachuri of Mahismati probably defeated them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dandin">Dandin</a>'s Dashakumaracharita, written about 125 years after the fall, tells of a son of Harishena who gave himself up to pleasure and was killed in a battle on the Wardha, while a Kadamba record suggests that the Kadambas annexed the kingdom. No Vakataka coins have been found.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vakataka dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their land grants, feudatory titles and royal ideology became models for the <b>Vakataka dynasty</b>, the Ikshvaku dynasty, the Pallava dynasty and other successors.</blockquote><a href="/empire/satavahana-dynasty">Satavahana dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Royal women could hold real power: the Licchavi princess Kumaradevi appears beside her husband Chandragupta I on Gupta gold coins, and Chandragupta II’s daughter Prabhavatigupta ruled the <b>Vakataka</b> kingdom as regent for about twenty years from around 390, issuing land grants in her own name.</blockquote><a href="/societies/classical-india">Social Hierarchy of Classical India</a></li><li><blockquote>Many scholars identify them with the Bharashivas of <b>Vakataka</b> records.</blockquote><a href="/state/nagas-of-padmavati">Nagas of Padmavati</a></li></ul></section>
